45417] [17592186083017 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5667" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083017 #edb/ref 1071 "$5!\\,2!=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083017 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ad07c38-8ace-5900-818d-34ccacc8ee11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083017 #edb/ref 1074 "The given expression is a product of two factorials.\n\n- Computing $5$! using the definition, we get\n$5! = 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 120$.\n- Computing $2$! using the definition, we get\n$2! = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2$.\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5!2! &= 5! \\cdot 2! \\\\\n&= 120 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 240\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083018 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5668" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083018 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $\\dfrac {5!} {2!}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083018 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22af250d-d219-5003-b972-aa912a0d6ee1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083018 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that\n\n$$\n5! = 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2! \\,\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $2$! that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {5!} {2!} &= \\dfrac {5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2!} {2!} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\cancel{2!}} {\\cancel{2!}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3} {1} \\\\[5pt] &= 60 \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083137 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9519" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083137 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\dfrac{5!}{0!}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083137 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "95e6de18-9036-55d2-a1cc-1be931c76d3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083137 #edb/ref 1074 "The given expression is a quotient of two factorials.\n\n- Computing $5$! using the definition, we get\n$5! = 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 120$.\n- By definition, the factorial of zero equals one:\n$0! = 1$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5!}{0!} &= \\f